1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,405
When you tap buy on one dollar of Apple
stock, Apple usually gets none of it.

2
00:00:04,455 --> 00:00:08,707
Your dollar goes somewhere else, and it
passes through several companies on the
way.

3
00:00:08,757 --> 00:00:12,920
This video follows that one dollar from
your pocket all the way to Wall Street.

4
00:00:12,970 --> 00:00:16,581
I'm the dollar, and nobody asked me if I
wanted to travel.

5
00:00:16,631 --> 00:00:19,889
By the end, you'll know exactly who ends
up holding your dollar.

6
00:00:19,939 --> 00:00:24,446
You'll also find out whose name is on your
stock, and it probably isn't yours.

7
00:00:24,496 --> 00:00:28,909
He was perfectly safe in here with me, and
now he wants adventure.

8
00:00:28,959 --> 00:00:32,217
And you'll get three checks worth doing
before you ever tap buy.

9
00:00:32,267 --> 00:00:35,723
Once you see this route, the buy button
won't look so simple again.

10
00:00:35,773 --> 00:00:40,629
This trip has five stops, and it's one
common route, since real orders can skip
some.

11
00:00:40,679 --> 00:00:44,108
Five stops sounds like a lot of walking
for one dollar.

12
00:00:44,158 --> 00:00:48,188
Before the dollar moves, you need to know
what it's actually buying.

13
00:00:48,238 --> 00:00:51,888
Picture a company as a pizza that's been
cut into slices.

14
00:00:51,938 --> 00:00:56,888
Each slice is a small piece of ownership,
and that slice is called a share.

15
00:00:56,938 --> 00:01:00,948
The stock market is simply the place where
people trade those slices.

16
00:01:00,998 --> 00:01:05,388
Here's a simple example to show how slices
work, with made-up numbers.

17
00:01:05,438 --> 00:01:10,628
Say a pizza shop is worth one hundred
dollars and is cut into one hundred
slices.

18
00:01:10,678 --> 00:01:15,548
Each slice is worth one dollar, so owning
one slice means owning one percent.

19
00:01:15,598 --> 00:01:20,528
If the shop grows to two hundred dollars,
your slice is worth two dollars.

20
00:01:20,578 --> 00:01:25,618
If the shop shrinks to fifty dollars, your
slice is worth fifty cents.

21
00:01:25,668 --> 00:01:29,258
Fifty cents is exactly why I keep my coins
at home.

22
00:01:29,308 --> 00:01:32,558
Now comes the part where a price tag can
fool you.

23
00:01:32,608 --> 00:01:38,508
Say Company A's stock costs five hundred
dollars a share, and Company B's costs
five dollars.

24
00:01:38,568 --> 00:01:43,658
The five-hundred-dollar share looks like
the bigger company, and that's the trap.

25
00:01:43,708 --> 00:01:49,148
Company A is cut into only three slices,
so its whole pizza is worth fifteen
hundred dollars.

26
00:01:49,988 --> 00:01:55,658
Company B is cut into a million slices, so
its whole pizza is worth five million.

27
00:01:55,708 --> 00:01:59,298
The cheap slice came from the far bigger
pizza.

28
00:01:59,348 --> 00:02:04,238
Price times the number of shares is called
market cap, the company's market value.

29
00:02:04,288 --> 00:02:10,328
Apple is cut into about fourteen and a
half billion shares, which is how its
value reaches trillions.

30
00:02:10,378 --> 00:02:14,308
So I'm buying a crumb of a slice, and I'm
still proud of it.

31
00:02:14,358 --> 00:02:17,208
Stop one is the investing app on your
phone.

32
00:02:17,258 --> 00:02:20,788
Say it's Tuesday morning, and you buy one
dollar of Apple.

33
00:02:20,838 --> 00:02:25,588
One Apple share costs a few hundred
dollars, so your dollar buys a crumb.

34
00:02:25,638 --> 00:02:29,868
Many big apps sell these crumbs, and
they're called fractional shares.

35
00:02:29,918 --> 00:02:34,568
You place a market order, which means buy
right away at the available price.

36
00:02:34,618 --> 00:02:38,448
The price you saw on screen can change
before your order goes through.

37
00:02:38,498 --> 00:02:42,428
Your broker may also bundle your crumb
with other customers' orders before it
buys.

38
00:02:42,478 --> 00:02:48,318
Your broker can send that order to an
exchange or a market maker, or fill it
itself.

39
00:02:48,368 --> 00:02:52,468
I've been turned into paperwork, and
nobody even said thank you.

40
00:02:52,518 --> 00:02:56,508
Stop two is a kind of company most people
have never heard of.

41
00:02:56,558 --> 00:03:00,128
Many retail orders don't go straight to a
stock exchange.

42
00:03:00,178 --> 00:03:04,648
Brokers often send them to a trading firm
called a market maker instead.

43
00:03:04,698 --> 00:03:09,328
Picture a currency booth at the airport,
with one price to buy and one to sell.

44
00:03:09,378 --> 00:03:14,748
A market maker works the same way,
regularly posting a buy price and a sell
price.

45
00:03:14,798 --> 00:03:19,718
Say it will buy Apple at three hundred
fifty dollars and sell at three hundred
fifty and two cents.

46
00:03:20,018 --> 00:03:25,548
That two-cent gap is called the spread,
and the market maker tries to earn it.

47
00:03:25,598 --> 00:03:29,968
It can lose money too, because the price
can move against it a minute later.

48
00:03:30,018 --> 00:03:35,828
Citadel Securities says it executes about
a third of all U.S. retail stock trading
volume.

49
00:03:35,878 --> 00:03:40,828
Market makers often pay brokers for
sending them orders, which is called
payment for order flow.

50
00:03:40,878 --> 00:03:45,058
That's one reason many apps charge no
commission on stock trades.

51
00:03:45,108 --> 00:03:52,628
Your broker still has to try to get you
the best available deal, considering price
and how reliably the order can be filled.

52
00:03:52,678 --> 00:03:56,628
Everyone on this trip gets paid except the
pig, apparently.

53
00:03:56,678 --> 00:04:01,688
If the market maker has the shares, it can
sell them straight from its own inventory.

54
00:04:01,738 --> 00:04:04,848
If it doesn't, your order can head to stop
three.

55
00:04:04,898 --> 00:04:10,408
Stop three is the stock exchange, the
place most people picture when they hear
Wall Street.

56
00:04:10,458 --> 00:04:16,888
The New York Stock Exchange still rings
its opening bell on Wall Street at nine
thirty in the morning, Eastern time.

57
00:04:16,938 --> 00:04:21,828
The computers that match its trades sit in
a data center in Mahwah, New Jersey.

58
00:04:21,878 --> 00:04:25,458
Nasdaq's main U.S. data center sits in
Carteret, New Jersey.

59
00:04:25,508 --> 00:04:29,548
I came all this way for Wall Street, and I
got New Jersey.

60
00:04:29,598 --> 00:04:33,188
An exchange works like a matchmaker that
keeps two lists.

61
00:04:33,238 --> 00:04:37,068
One list holds buyers, and the other list
holds sellers.

62
00:04:37,118 --> 00:04:41,768
When a buyer and a seller agree on a
price, the exchange matches them.

63
00:04:41,818 --> 00:04:46,408
Regular trading runs from nine thirty to
four, Eastern time, Monday through Friday.

64
00:04:46,458 --> 00:04:50,108
Market holidays and a few early-close days
are the exceptions.

65
00:04:50,158 --> 00:04:54,228
Some apps let you trade earlier or later,
when far fewer people are trading.

66
00:04:54,278 --> 00:05:00,138
Fewer traders can mean bigger price jumps
and a wider gap between buy and sell
prices.

67
00:05:00,188 --> 00:05:04,858
Quick check, because look at how far your
dollar has already come.

68
00:05:04,908 --> 00:05:08,798
Stop one turned your dollar into an order
inside your app.

69
00:05:08,848 --> 00:05:13,558
Stop two was a market maker, which may
sell to you from its own shelf.

70
00:05:13,608 --> 00:05:17,758
Stop three was an exchange, which matches
buyers with sellers.

71
00:05:17,808 --> 00:05:21,268
The next stop is where your dollar
actually changes hands.

72
00:05:21,318 --> 00:05:27,138
Pick how much of your dollar Apple gets,
all of it, half, or none.

73
00:05:29,088 --> 00:05:33,238
Usually none of it, because Apple isn't
the one selling you the share.

74
00:05:33,288 --> 00:05:36,758
Think of a concert ticket that gets resold
between fans.

75
00:05:36,808 --> 00:05:40,638
The band gets paid when the box office
sells the ticket the first time.

76
00:05:40,688 --> 00:05:45,498
When you buy that ticket later from
another fan, your money goes to the fan.

77
00:05:45,548 --> 00:05:49,338
Apple's box-office moment came when it
first sold shares to the public.

78
00:05:49,388 --> 00:05:54,038
That happened on December twelfth,
nineteen eighty, at twenty-two dollars a
share.

79
00:05:54,088 --> 00:05:57,118
Today, your dollar goes to whoever sold
you the share.

80
00:05:57,168 --> 00:06:03,058
It could be your own broker, a market
maker, a big fund, or a retired teacher.

81
00:06:03,108 --> 00:06:07,138
Say the retired teacher is selling a
little stock to help pay for a new roof.

82
00:06:07,188 --> 00:06:11,188
Tell her I'll pay for the roof myself, so
she doesn't have to sell.

83
00:06:11,238 --> 00:06:17,128
Your dollar buys a crumb of her stock, so
your dollar ends up helping pay for that
roof.

84
00:06:17,178 --> 00:06:22,028
Promise one, done, because your dollar
went to the seller, not to Apple.

85
00:06:22,078 --> 00:06:27,428
Apple still cares about its price, partly
because its executives are often paid in
stock.

86
00:06:27,478 --> 00:06:30,588
Stop five is where your trade becomes
official.

87
00:06:30,638 --> 00:06:34,528
Your trade is matched, but the paperwork
isn't finished yet.

88
00:06:34,578 --> 00:06:40,608
Since May twenty-eighth, twenty twenty-
four, most U.S. stock trades settle one
business day later.

89
00:06:40,658 --> 00:06:44,788
That means your Tuesday trade is usually
final on Wednesday.

90
00:06:44,838 --> 00:06:50,228
Behind the scenes, companies in a group
called the D-T-C-C clear and settle the
trade.

91
00:06:50,278 --> 00:06:56,298
They guarantee eligible trades, work out
what each firm owes, and hold the shares.

92
00:06:56,348 --> 00:07:00,708
A referee for money is the first sensible
thing I've heard today.

93
00:07:00,758 --> 00:07:04,448
Now for the paperwork question from the
start of this video.

94
00:07:04,498 --> 00:07:09,968
Many shares held through brokerages are
registered under one name, Cede and
Company.

95
00:07:10,018 --> 00:07:14,968
Cede and Company is the name the
Depository Trust Company uses on the
official record.

96
00:07:15,018 --> 00:07:17,488
It's a bit like a coat check at a
restaurant.

97
00:07:17,538 --> 00:07:22,428
The hanger tag doesn't have your name, but
your ticket proves the coat belongs to
you.

98
00:07:22,478 --> 00:07:27,568
Your brokerage's records are that ticket,
and they list you as the beneficial owner.

99
00:07:27,618 --> 00:07:33,528
Promise two, done, because the name on the
record isn't yours, but the shares are.

100
00:07:33,578 --> 00:07:36,728
The gains are yours, and so are the
losses.

101
00:07:36,778 --> 00:07:39,918
Voting with a fractional share depends on
your broker's rules.

102
00:07:39,968 --> 00:07:44,258
Quick check before the big part, because
the whole route is on the map now.

103
00:07:44,308 --> 00:07:49,078
Your dollar became an order, took one
possible route, and reached a seller.

104
00:07:49,128 --> 00:07:53,258
It settled a business day later, with Cede
and Company on the record.

105
00:07:53,308 --> 00:07:58,718
The order itself can go through in a
fraction of a second, and settlement
follows a business day later.

106
00:07:58,768 --> 00:08:04,598
Now comes the part that matters most,
which is how one dollar turns into more
dollars.

107
00:08:04,648 --> 00:08:08,038
There are two main ways to make money from
owning a stock.

108
00:08:08,088 --> 00:08:10,838
The first is selling for more than you
paid.

109
00:08:10,888 --> 00:08:13,978
Here's a made-up example to show how that
works.

110
00:08:14,028 --> 00:08:19,048
Say Maya buys ten shares at two hundred
dollars each, which costs two thousand
dollars.

111
00:08:19,448 --> 00:08:24,618
A year later, Jordan offers two hundred
fifty dollars a share for all ten.

112
00:08:24,668 --> 00:08:29,198
Maya sells and makes five hundred dollars,
before taxes and any fees.

113
00:08:29,248 --> 00:08:33,938
If Jordan had offered one hundred fifty
dollars a share, Maya would have lost five
hundred.

114
00:08:33,988 --> 00:08:39,758
The second way is dividends, when a
company shares part of its profits with
its owners.

115
00:08:39,808 --> 00:08:43,858
Not every company pays dividends, and the
ones that do can cut them.

116
00:08:43,908 --> 00:08:48,458
Dividends sound like savings that pay you
rent, and I approve.

117
00:08:48,508 --> 00:08:54,318
The price you usually see is the last-
traded price, the last price someone
actually paid.

118
00:08:54,368 --> 00:08:58,078
Say Apple's last trade was three hundred
fifty dollars even.

119
00:08:58,128 --> 00:09:03,438
The cheapest seller wants three hundred
fifty dollars and five cents, and a buyer
pays it.

120
00:09:03,488 --> 00:09:06,368
The last-trade price now becomes three
hundred fifty dollars and five cents.

121
00:09:08,068 --> 00:09:13,158
Millions of small deals like that, every
day, are what make a stock chart move.

122
00:09:13,208 --> 00:09:18,418
Prices mostly move on what buyers and
sellers expect a company to earn later.

123
00:09:18,468 --> 00:09:23,558
Good news can lift a stock's price in
minutes, and bad news can sink it.

124
00:09:23,608 --> 00:09:29,618
In his nineteen eighty-seven letter,
Warren Buffett passed on a lesson from his
teacher, Benjamin Graham.

125
00:09:29,668 --> 00:09:36,078
In the short run, the market is a voting
machine, but in the long run it is a
weighing machine.

126
00:09:36,128 --> 00:09:41,458
Day to day, prices follow moods, and over
many years they tend to follow profits.

127
00:09:41,508 --> 00:09:45,498
Picking single companies isn't the only
way to own stocks.

128
00:09:45,548 --> 00:09:51,798
An S and P five hundred index fund is a
basket holding slices of about five
hundred companies.

129
00:09:51,848 --> 00:09:56,018
You buy a slice of the fund, and the fund
owns the companies for you.

130
00:09:56,068 --> 00:09:59,498
The biggest companies get the biggest
weight inside the basket.

131
00:09:59,548 --> 00:10:04,498
I'm spread across five hundred companies
now, and I've never felt so thin.

132
00:10:04,548 --> 00:10:10,058
A basket holding five hundred companies is
the first investment I'd happily sit in.

133
00:10:10,108 --> 00:10:16,478
Over long periods, the S and P five
hundred has historically returned roughly
ten percent a year.

134
00:10:16,528 --> 00:10:22,698
That's with dividends reinvested and
before inflation, and it's history, not a
promise.

135
00:10:22,748 --> 00:10:26,238
Here's what a steady ten percent a year
would do to one dollar.

136
00:10:26,288 --> 00:10:31,928
After forty years of steady ten percent
growth, one dollar becomes about forty-
five dollars.

137
00:10:32,308 --> 00:10:36,758
Real markets don't move in a straight
line, and the drops can be brutal.

138
00:10:36,808 --> 00:10:43,298
From late two thousand seven to early two
thousand nine, the S and P five hundred
lost more than half its value.

139
00:10:43,348 --> 00:10:49,498
In twenty twenty, it fell almost thirty-
four percent between February nineteenth
and March twenty-third.

140
00:10:49,548 --> 00:10:54,718
The broad index came back both times, but
a single company doesn't always come back.

141
00:10:54,768 --> 00:10:58,378
This is exactly why I sleep with one eye
open.

142
00:10:58,428 --> 00:11:02,658
Here are the three checks worth doing
before you ever tap buy.

143
00:11:02,708 --> 00:11:07,518
Check one is the size of the company, not
just the price of one share.

144
00:11:07,568 --> 00:11:12,658
Price times shares is the number that
tells you how big the pizza really is.

145
00:11:12,708 --> 00:11:17,678
Check two is how your order works, since a
market order takes the available price.

146
00:11:17,728 --> 00:11:21,538
Your app should explain its order types
and how it routes your orders.

147
00:11:21,588 --> 00:11:26,358
Check three is whether your brokerage is
an S-I-P-C member, and what that covers.

148
00:11:26,408 --> 00:11:32,818
If a member firm fails with customer
assets missing, S-I-P-C can help recover
them.

149
00:11:32,868 --> 00:11:39,238
Protection goes up to five hundred
thousand dollars per customer, including
two hundred fifty thousand in cash.

150
00:11:39,288 --> 00:11:43,388
That protection is for a failed brokerage,
not for a stock that falls.

151
00:11:43,438 --> 00:11:47,578
Nobody insures my feelings either, and
I've been through a lot today.

152
00:11:47,628 --> 00:11:52,868
These checks keep money safe, which makes
this the only list I trust.

153
00:11:52,918 --> 00:11:57,678
That's promise three, and notice that none
of these checks tells you what to buy.

154
00:11:57,728 --> 00:12:03,118
The whole trip is on the map now, from
your pocket to a retired teacher's new
roof.

155
00:12:03,168 --> 00:12:07,838
Apple never got your dollar, but you now
own a tiny piece of Apple.

156
00:12:07,888 --> 00:12:11,518
Before I ask you something, I'll go first
with my own story.

157
00:12:11,568 --> 00:12:16,558
The first stock I ever bought was Nvidia,
one of the biggest companies in the
market.

158
00:12:16,608 --> 00:12:22,218
I picked it because I think it's tied to
where the world is heading, and that's my
reason, not advice.

159
00:12:22,268 --> 00:12:25,978
The first time I opened a candlestick
chart, I was completely lost.

160
00:12:26,028 --> 00:12:30,418
I started learning about money because I
want to be financially free.

161
00:12:30,468 --> 00:12:34,968
I offered to pay for that roof, and
somehow I ended up inside it.

162
00:12:35,018 --> 00:12:39,948
Next time, we'll break down how to read a
candlestick chart without getting lost.

163
00:12:39,998 --> 00:12:44,768
Tell me in the comments which stop
surprised you most, and what your first
stock was.

164
00:12:44,818 --> 00:12:49,120
If this made the stock market make more
sense, a like really helps the channel.
